# False unused: property-syntax reads are not credited to their get_/set_ accessor

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Reported 2026-08-30 by session tm-tournament-station-f7 from the tm-tournament-station tree, verified against `src/` before filing.

## Defect

A read written in AngelScript property syntax is not credited to the accessor
that implements it, so the accessor is reported `a function 'get_X' is never
used` even where it is read from production files.

Two distinct forms, and they must BOTH be covered — fixing only the first
leaves the second live:

1. **Namespace-qualified**: `Directives::Showing` read in `Main.as` does not
   mark `Directives::get_Showing` used.
2. **Member on an instance**: `c.IsSecure` where the class declares
   `bool get_IsSecure()`.

## Confirmed instances (reporter's tree, all read outside test/dev files)

| accessor | read from |
|---|---|
| `Directives::get_Showing` | Main.as, Messages.as |
| `Directives::get_HistoryCount` | Main.as |
| `Messages::get_PendingCount` | Main.as |
| `Session::get_IsConnected` | Main.as, Exports.as |
| `Session::get_Status` | Main.as |
| `Session::get_TournamentName` | Main.as, Branding.as, Directives.as |

Not a threshold effect: `Directives::Showing` has 13 reads and is still
reported unused.

## Why (coordinator, pre-dispatch)

The machinery EXISTS and some path is not reaching it. `src/analysis/unused.rs`
contains no `get_`/`set_` handling at all (1303 lines, zero matches), but
`src/typecheck/checker.rs` has both:

- `record_value_use_variants` (:802) records `name`, `get_name`, `set_name`
- `record_member_use_from_type` (:816) records `Type::member`,
  `Type::get_member`, `Type::set_member` across the whole inheritance chain

So this is an INCONSISTENCY, not an unimplemented feature — which means the
fix is to route the missing paths through the existing helpers, not to invent
a new mechanism. Find why a namespace-qualified read does not reach
`record_value_use_variants`, and why the member form does not reach
`record_member_use_from_type` (receiver type unresolved? class-name
normalization failing? a different code path entirely?). Do not add a third
recorder.

Note the deliberate comment on `record_member_use_from_type`: it over-marks
rather than under-marks, because "a false 'unused' is far worse than a missed
warning". That principle decides any ambiguity in this ticket.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

Red: a namespace `N` with `bool get_P()`, read as `N::P` from another file ->
currently reports `get_P` unused.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize — the reporter's two forms are the
minimum, not the set):
- namespace-qualified READ `N::P` -> marks `get_P`
- namespace-qualified WRITE `N::P = x` -> marks `set_P`
- read-modify-write `N::P += 1` -> marks BOTH
- member on instance `obj.P` -> marks `Type::get_P`
- member write `obj.P = x` -> marks `Type::set_P`
- through a handle `objHandle.P`
- inherited accessor declared on a PARENT class
- accessor declared in a dep/export, read from the dependent plugin
- getter-only property written to -> still an error, this must not silence it
- a genuinely unused `get_P` (no reads anywhere) -> STILL reported. This is the
  gate against over-marking everything to make the FP go away.
- `this.P` inside the declaring class (already works per :1196 docstring —
  pin it so the fix does not regress it)

## Gate

FP fixture ERROR/WARNING counts must not RISE. A DROP is expected here and is
the win. Measure two binaries on the same machine at the same time with
absolute paths, both plain and json.

## Related

Reporter's own correction, worth recording: they nearly filed ~19 of 24
warnings as false positives, and on checking found only 6 were. The other 13
were production functions whose only callers live in `*_Test.as`/`*_Dev.as`,
which a release build excludes — genuinely unused, and their own code smell.
Do not widen this ticket into 'unused analysis is too aggressive'. It is
specifically the property-syntax/accessor mapping.

## Discriminating gate (reporter, 2026-08-30) — USE THIS, it is the whole test

The reporting tree contains BOTH halves already classified, which makes it a
ready-made discriminator rather than just a source of examples. Repo:
`~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-tournament-station` at master; `just lsp`
reproduces exactly 24 warnings; the breakdown is in its `AGENTS.md`.

- **6 must DISAPPEAR** — the table above, including `c.IsSecure` (member form).
- **18 must SURVIVE** — 13 production functions whose only callers live in
  `*_Test.as`/`*_Dev.as` (excluded from a release build, so genuinely unused),
  4 export-surface functions that are B062's business and NOT this ticket's,
  and 1 with no caller anywhere (`Branding::get_Logo`).

**The part that makes it a real gate: 8 of those 13 are THEMSELVES `get_`
accessors** — `Directives::get_Latest`, `get_PendingCount`, `get_ShownAt`,
`get_HasSnoozed`, `Messages::get_Showing`, `MatchGuard::get_Current`,
`get_MsInCurrent`, `Transport::get_IsSecure`.

So the lazy fix — stop warning on accessors, or mark every `get_X` used
whenever `X` appears anywhere — PASSES the six-row table and silently eats
eight correct warnings in the same repo. Same-shaped symbol, opposite expected
outcome, one tree. A fix is only correct if it moves 24 -> 18, not 24 -> 10.

Note `Transport::get_IsSecure` appears in BOTH lists: read as `c.IsSecure` from
production (must disappear) while a different `Transport` accessor is test-only
(must survive). Confirm which is which against the tree before asserting
either — do not assume the name alone decides it.